welcome to the Toyota green room we are  talking your heart health with dr. taryn  Rula welcome yes my favorite topic  it's your favorite topic and why are we  not as knowledgeable as we should be in  terms of our bodies and how our heart is  impacted by the everyday stresses that  we deal with I mean why don't we know  this is the number one killer of women I  know and despite the fact that we've  been educating women since 2004 with the  Go Red For Women campaign we still have  this big gap and awareness women just  don't think about heart disease and  stroke as a threat to them they think  about cancer a lot of times but when you  look at the statistics about one out of  three women will die of heart disease  one out of thirty will die of breast  cancer more women die of cardiovascular  disease than all forms of cancer  combined so it really is a big problem  that more women don't appreciate that  but in addition they don't really know  the symptoms or how to recognize them  they don't understand risk factors and  how to manage them so we have a lot more  education we need to do oh we doing  better though we're doing better no  doubt and I think a lot of these  educational and awareness campaigns have  accomplished a lot in terms of saving  women and making them healthier but we  still have much more to do so that's why  the days like this where we wear red and  raise awareness and tell our stories  it's really important so what should  women what are the signs right so a lot  of times unfortunately with heart  disease it can be silent and that's one  of the things that makes it so deadly so  you can feel well and you can have high  blood pressure or high cholesterol or  diabetes or plaque in the arteries that  supply blood to your heart and you don't  know you have it until the day you have  your first heart attack or stroke so  that makes it difficult and I think  that's one of the problems with  cardiovascular disease but if you do  have symptoms by far and away chest pain  most common symptom for women but it  doesn't have to be the kind where you  flex your chest and fall over on the  floor and it's horrible it's subtle it  can be subtle it can be kind of up here  in the chest it can be more down here  like indigestion right so some people  say I have indigestion and that's all it  is and they don't really get it checked  out but then women can also get things  like shortness of breath fatigue  lightheadedness feeling kind of sweaty  or clammy arm pain jaw pain or back  so some of these other symptoms that may  not make them think I should have my  heart checked out those are also  symptoms or what we associate with  anxiety it can be and I mean I had I  can't tell you how many women have come  into my office and said that they've  seen other doctors and their doctor told  them that they're just anxious or  stressed yesterday I had a woman in my  office and she said I can't walk more  than three blocks before getting short  of breath and I'm coming to see you  because the last person I saw told me  I'm depressed and I need to see a  psychiatrist so unfortunately in the  medical community we have not always  taken women's complaint seriously and  that has contributed to the problem at  what age should we start seriously  taking a look at our vitals early  earlier than I think a lot of people  think about so the recommendation by the  American Heart Association for example  is to start having your blood pressure  and your cholesterol checked as early as  age 20 so a lot of people in their 20s  and 30s are not thinking about this and  one thing I think that women can really  do that would be helpful is what happens  to women during pregnancy can be a  marker of their future risk really yes  and so we know women go every year to  get their pap smear they get care you  know in with their OB guide and that's a  great time to think about what might be  happening with my cardiovascular system  so if they have preeclampsia or  gestational diabetes or gestational high  blood pressure or even babies that are  low birth weight all of these things can  signal that they might be at increased  risk down the line so the the ob/gyns  office is actually a good place for  women to start the conversation is that  because of the stress of the baby it's  like a stress test exactly exactly so I  want to know for for someone who has  notoriously low blood pressure that's  good cholesterol in check and all of a  sudden you go to the doctor and you see  all of a sudden a spike in your blood  pressure a spike in your cholesterol  measuring and you've hit that certain  age is that the time when you need to be  concerned and say okay this is where I  really need the rubber meets the road  yeah I mean cardiovascular disease is  all about prevention prevention  prevention because it takes time in some  cases for the problem to occur so for  plaque to buildup in the arteries it  doesn't happen overnight it's over years  and so  all the things that you're doing ahead  of time to kind of prepare and plan and  keep your numbers down and in the right  range are really important to prevent  that first event like a heart attack or  stroke remember happening so certainly  we know that risk for women goes up over  the age of 55 or when they're  postmenopausal that's kind of the age  cutoff and then we think about things  like family history so you may have a  normal blood pressure and great  cholesterol levels but if you have a  very strong family history of early  heart disease that's something that we  need to know as your doctor because it  plays into your risk and if your parents  or grandparents died from the disease  yep so we look at the whole family tree  but in particular we pay attention to  what we call first-degree relatives  meaning your parents or your siblings  and we look to see did a woman a  first-degree relative have an early  heart attack or stroke before the age of  65 did a man have an early heart attack  or stroke before the age of 55 and if  they did that's a marker for high risk  what are the biggest risk factors you  can avoid so high blood pressure  one of the biggest abnormal cholesterol  levels so a high LDL or bad cholesterol  a low HDL or good cholesterol diabetes  diabetes is a big big risk factor and  then smoking so those are kind of the  big ones and then you think about things  like obesity lack of exercise stress too  much alcohol these are kind of the  traditional ones that we look at what's  interesting for women is they have a lot  of these ones that we don't often talk  about that can put them at higher risk  so things like polycystic ovarian  syndrome we talked about pregnancy  issues early menopause migraines with  aura women who have autoimmune disorders  like lupus or rheumatoid arthritis or  even psoriatic arthritis all of these  things can and mental health I didn't  mention but very important depression  anxiety these can all play into  elevating a woman's risk of  cardiovascular disease and why is that  we don't know for a lot of these reasons  the exact pathophysiology or mechanisms  we're learning about it you know in some  cases for example with stress we know  that you have a release of a lot of  these fight-or-flight hormones stress  hormones like cortisol that can increase  inflammation that can increase your  blood pressure and your heart rate so  for some of them we have an idea of the  pathway for others the of the those risk  factors we're still figuring it out  what's the best thing you can do for  yourself the best thing you can do is to  have  a what we used to call it or what we  call a well woman exam where you  basically go to see your doctor you know  you find out your numbers and you talk  about your risk you have your doctor sit  down and say with you Michelle you know  your risk of a heart attack or stroke in  the next 10 years is 2% or it's 20% and  then you figure out a plan what do you  need to do I need to lose weight I need  to exercise whatever it is for you and  sometimes it includes medication things  like aspirin or cholesterol-lowering  medications or drugs to treat  hypertension so but really I think it's  just putting the idea in your head that  this is something I need to pay  attention to even if I feel well even if  my numbers are good I should have this  on my radar at the top of my list of  things that should be a priority because  there's no quick fix there's no quick  fix and unfortunately women tend to  prioritize themselves last last on the  list you know husband kids work  everything else is first and they really  many times ignore a lot of the symptoms  they're having for a while and that can  be problematic because for example you  know we know that this damage can happen  over time for a lot of these diseases  and then once you have a heart attack  those cells start to die they don't come  back  they don't regenerate and so you form  scar tissue that can put you at risk for  heart failure so always better to catch  things early errs to start to treat them  earlier prevention is key it is glass of  wine in there - I actually think we do  say that as a woman you're allowed one  drink a day really you are and so I  actually tend to tell my patients if  they're going to choose a drink red wine  is a good choice because it has  flavonoids in it which is the same  compound in dark chocolate that kind of  tends to help blood vessel health dark  chocolate good - our chocolate and wine  can go tonight and that is your heart  healthy dr. taryn Rula thank you so much  Michelle
 

